{
  "title": "Passing a scalar torch.Tensor to an IntList is completely broken",
  "issue_id": "167562",
  "bug_description": "### 🐛 Describe the bug\n\nPythonArgParser accepts a scalar Tensor in places that expect an `IntList`. However, the parsing code casts the Tensor to a PyListObject. It just so happens that PyListObject `ob_size` is the same place as THPVariable's `bool isBorrowed_` from `MaybeOwned<Tensor>` so the parsing code thinks it's an empty list (!?) but none of this makes any sense.\n\nFor example, in eager mode:\n\n```python\n    x = torch.ones(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)\n    print(x.sum(dim=torch.tensor(3)))  # Ed, why are we accepting a scalar tensor here?!\n    print(x.sum(dim=3))\n```\n\nIt also shows up in places where we generate scalar FakeTensors, such as `random.randint()`:\n\n```python\nimport torch\nimport random\n\ndef foo():\n    x = torch.ones(2, 2, 2)\n    return x.sum(dim=random.randint(0, 0))  # i.e.,  dim=0\n\n@torch.compile\ndef foo_compile():\n    x = torch.ones(2, 2, 2)\n    return x.sum(dim=random.randint(0, 0))  # i.e.,  dim=0\n\nassert foo().shape == foo_compile().shape\n```\n\ncc @albanD @ezyang\n\n### Versions\n\nIt's on main as of Nov 11, 2025 (fad48ffa62e1e0a39ee52b649b7c27949772a965)",
  "apis": [
    "u003erandom.randint",
    "var.dtype",
